     <p><strong><p><strong>Jonathan Quong, <em>Political Philosophy: The Puzzle of Legitimate Injustice</em>, Princeton University Press, 2026, 216pp., $29.95 (hbk) ISBN 9780691215648.</strong></p></strong></p>
     <p><strong>Reviewed by Charles Larmore, Brown University</strong></p>
     <p>Jonathan Quong’s new book pursues two distinct goals. As a volume in the series “Princeton Foundations of Contemporary Philosophy”, it is intended to offer a survey of some of the key issues in political philosophy, one that will engage even non-specialists having little prior acquaintance with this field. Yet it seeks to do so by focusing on only one specific problem, canvasses a number of proposed ways of handling it, and goes on to present Quong’s own solution. This dual aim might have proven impracticable in a book of less than 200 pages if the problem chosen were not as fundamental as it is, ramifying into just about every important question in political philosophy.</p> <p>As the subtitle of Quong’s book indicates, the problem is that...
